@article{williams_climate_2025,
	title = {The climate change impact of surgical and non-surgical skin cancer appointments: a process-based life cycle assessment},
	shorttitle = {The climate change impact of surgical and non-surgical skin cancer appointments},
	abstract = {1. Goal of the study

The goal of this study is to estimate the climate change impact (in kg {CO}2-e) of different scenarios in which a patient attends a clinical consultation at a dermatology clinic for the diagnosis or management of skin cancer. We do not intend to make any comparative assessments. We are preparing this life cycle assessment ({LCA}) for an academic healthcare audience, including dermatologists, health services researchers, and health economists, to inform research and decisions regarding health system sustainability. The audience will not likely be familiar with {LCA} methodology. The work will be disclosed to the public and we will publish the work in an open access peer-reviewed health journal. We will conduct this {LCA} in accordance with International Standards {ISO} 14040:2006 and {ISO} 14044:2006 [1,2]. 

2. Study setting

This study will be conducted based on information from two skin-cancer focused subspecialty dermatology clinics: Sydney Melanoma Diagnostic Centre ({SMDC}) and Melanoma Dermatology ({MD}). {SMDC} private dermatology clinic based at Royal Prince Alfred Hospital in Camperdown, Sydney, New South Wales. {MD} is a privately operated dermatology clinic part of the Melanoma Institute Australia within the Poche Centre in Wollstonecraft, Sydney, New South Wales.

3. Functional units and reference flows

In this study, we will investigate the climate change impact of different scenarios in which patients engage with a dermatology clinic for the treatment and/or management of confirmed or suspected melanoma or other skin other cancer. These scenarios can be broadly grouped into two categories: 

(1)	non-surgical appointments for the identification and monitoring of skin to diagnose lesions suspicious for melanoma or other skin cancers, or 

(2)	surgical appointments for the excision of skin lesions suspected to be melanoma or other skin cancers.

Clinical consultations for the purpose of monitoring skin lesions typically rely on in-person history, naked-eye physical examination and dermoscopy, a non-invasive technique using a dermatoscope to visualise features of the skin not visible to the naked eye (i.e., a full skin examination). During these kinds of appointments patients may also undergo sequential dermoscopic digital imaging ({SDDI}) if any suspicious lesions are identified and immediate excision is not required [3]. 

Another type of clinical consultation for the purpose of monitoring skin lesions is total body photography ({TBP}). {TBP} is a technique where images of the entire body are taken, usually by a melanographer, to act as a baseline and to detect new or macroscopically changing skin lesions. Comparisons to these images can then be made during full skin examinations. {TBP} may take place during the same visit to the clinic as a full skin examination, or during a dedicated appointment.

Clinical consultations for the purpose of excising lesions suspicious for melanoma or other skin cancers are often arranged following non-surgical skin monitoring appointments. For lesions suspicious for melanoma, excisional biopsies are recommended. Shave and/or punch biopsies may also be conducted if clinical suspicion for melanoma is low, or for lesions suspected to be non-melanoma skin cancer [6]. These appointments may be  undertaken at the same, or on a separate visit to the clinic. 

In this study each scenario will be considered its own functional unit and no comparisons between reference flows will be made. Details are provided in Table 1 in the attached protocol.

4. Data sources

Data will be collected directly using custom data collection sheets developed in consultation with dermatologists and staff at the participating clinics. These will aim to record the number and types of items used during the different types of dermatology appointments. {SMDC} and {MD} are recruiting clinics for the {MEL}-{SELF} trial (described in detail elsewhere [5]), and additional data are available from the trial. The {MEL}-{SELF} trial was approved by Sydney Local Health District ({RPAH} Zone) Ethics Committee (2019/{ETH}0531). Publicly available data will also be used as necessary.

Life cycle analysis will be modelled using {SimaPro} Analyst Release 9.6.0.1 (Pre Sustainability, Amersfoot, the Netherlands). The {AusLCI} Version 2 (Lifecycles, Melbourne, Australia) and Ecoinvent Version 3.1 (Ecoinvent, Zurich, Switzerland) databases will be used to build the model, with preference given to Australian unit processes from the {AusLCI} database where possible. 

5. System boundary

For the functional units identified, we will include the following as appropriate:

•	The manufacture, use, and disposal of all single-use medical equipment
•	The manufacture, use, and sterilisation of all reusable medical equipment 
•	Patient transport required for in-person appointments
•	Energy use for duration of appointment, including for imaging (total body photography) machines
•	Energy use for transmission of imaging via teledermatology

We will exclude:

•	Staff travel to the skin cancer clinic
•	The manufacture and maintenance of clinic electricity infrastructure
•	Heating and cooling systems of the clinic
•	Healthcare resources that are used outside of the skin cancer clinic, including pathologist review of an excised lesion

System diagrams will be developed as appropriate for the final manuscript. 

6. Data collection and requirements

6.1. Medical equipment

Data collection sheets will be developed in consultation with clinic staff and/or by conducting an audit of each clinic’s storeroom and supply ordering invoices. Medical equipment will be investigated individually to determine composition, weights, and manufacture origin. The total number of each item used will be estimated from up to ten appointments of each type. Disposable medical equipment will likely include: gauze, drapes, gloves, syringes, needles, biopsy instruments, dressings, alcohol wipes, markers, and paper bed sheets.

Reusable medical equipment is often provided as ‘kits’ based on the type of excision being performed and are likely to contain a stainless-steel tray, scalpel handle, scissors, needle holders, and forceps. A kit will be obtained, and items will be investigated individually to determine composition and weight. The cleaning and sterilisation procedure will be investigated and included. We will determine and assume an average number of uses per item in consultation with clinic staff and procedure logs.

For all items, the country of manufacture will be determined and the transport (i.e., shipping) of the item to Sydney will be estimated and included in the model.

6.2. Patient travel

Patient travel from their home to the clinic will be estimated using data available from the {MEL}-{SELF} trial. This includes information about the mode of transport, and distance travelled for each appointment made during the study period. The model will be constructed to include different modes of transport (i.e., car, bus, train, bike, and walk).

6.3. Electricity use

Energy consumed by any total body photography imaging machines will be measured using a power meter and/or power meter and logger over a convenient sampling period. Power from computers, monitors, dermatoscopes, and other general equipment used during appointments will be estimated using published data. 

6.4. Time

Data collection sheets will collect information about the start and end time of each type of appointment.},
	journaltitle = {Open Science Framework},
	author = {Williams, Jake T W and Bell, K and Morton, R L and Guitera, P and Martin, L and {McAlister}, S},
	date = {2025},
	langid = {english},
	file = {Snapshot:/Users/jwil6086/Zotero/storage/WZRGCMKM/e8cx2.html:text/html},
}

@article{barraclough_carbon_2025,
	title = {Carbon Emissions From Different Dialysis Modalities: A Life Cycle Assessment},
	volume = {86},
	issn = {0272-6386},
	url = {https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0272638625009205},
	doi = {10.1053/j.ajkd.2025.04.019},
	shorttitle = {Carbon Emissions From Different Dialysis Modalities},
	abstract = {Rationale \& Objective
As the health impacts of climate change intensify, the health care sector faces growing pressure to minimize its carbon footprint. Achieving this goal requires understanding of the carbon impact of widely used medical treatments. This study evaluated and compared the carbon footprints of in-center hemodialysis, home hemodialysis, automated peritoneal dialysis ({APD}), and continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis ({CAPD}).
Study Design
Comparative life cycle assessment.
Setting \& Participants
In-center hemodialysis, home-based hemodialysis, {CAPD}, and {APD} treatments provided by the Royal Melbourne Hospital Kidney Care Service, Melbourne, Victoria, Australia.
Outcome
Carbon emissions in kilograms of carbon dioxide equivalents (kg {CO}2e) from the annual provision of dialysis to a single patient.
Analytical Approach
Attributional process-based comparative life cycle assessment methodology was used to identify the sources and extent of carbon emissions for all dialysis modalities.
Results
In-center hemodialysis had the highest annual per patient carbon impact, at 4,814kg {CO}2e. In comparison, emissions from home hemodialysis were 41\% lower (2,938kg {CO}2e), {APD} 29\% lower (3,339kg {CO}2e), and {CAPD} 59\% lower (1,969kg {CO}2e). Across all 4 modalities, consumables were the largest source of emissions. For in-center hemodialysis, patient travel was the main factor contributing to its higher carbon footprint compared with home-based dialysis options.
Limitations
Single dialysis provider in an urban setting.
Conclusions
In-center hemodialysis has a substantially higher carbon footprint than all home dialysis options, informing the choice of home dialysis when feasible and appropriate for individual patients.
Plain-Language Summary
As climate change intensifies, there is growing pressure on health care systems to reduce their greenhouse gas emissions. To do this effectively, it is important to understand the carbon footprint of different treatment options and identify key areas where emissions are highest. This study compared greenhouse gas emissions from 4 types of dialysis: hemodialysis performed in a health care facility (in-center hemodialysis), home hemodialysis, automated peritoneal dialysis ({APD}), and continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis ({CAPD}). We found that in-center hemodialysis had the highest carbon footprint, primarily due to the impact of patient travel. Among the home therapies, {APD} was responsible for higher emissions than home hemodialysis while {CAPD} had the lowest carbon footprint. Across all treatment types, consumables were the largest source of carbon emissions.},
	pages = {465--474.e1},
	number = {4},
	journaltitle = {American Journal of Kidney Diseases},
	shortjournal = {American Journal of Kidney Diseases},
